SH(1)                          P32W Unix                           SH(1)

NAME
       sh - command interpreter (shell)

SYNOPSIS
       sh
       sh file [arg ...]
       sh -c command [arg ...]

DESCRIPTION
       sh is the P32W Unix command language interpreter. Run with no
       arguments it reads commands from the standard input; when that
       is a terminal it prompts, edits lines and keeps a history (see
       LINE EDITING and HISTORY below). With a file argument it reads
       the commands from file, which is a shell script, and finishes
       at its end. With -c it runs the command text (which may hold
       several lines) and finishes.

       The prompt is "$ ", or "# " when the effective user is root.
       While a construct (if, while, for, case, a function, ( ) or
       { }) is still open the shell prompts "> " for the rest of it
       and runs nothing until it is complete.

       A login shell (one started as -sh, as login(1) and su -
       start it) first runs the commands in /etc/profile and then
       $HOME/.profile, if they exist.

       COMMANDS

       A simple command is a sequence of words separated by blanks;
       the first word is the command name, the rest its arguments.
       A name containing / is run as given; any other is looked for
       in each directory of $PATH in turn (/bin:/usr/bin if PATH is
       not set). If it is not found the shell prints "sh: name: not
       found" and the status is 127. A file starting with #! runs
       the interpreter named there (#!/bin/sh, #!/usr/bin/lua), and
       a text file without #! is run by sh, so a script made
       executable (chmod 755) runs by name like a program.

       A pipeline is one or more commands separated by |; the
       standard output of each is connected to the standard input of
       the next, and the shell waits for the last. Its status is the
       exit status of the last command, or 128 plus the signal number
       if that was killed by a signal (the shell then says "memory
       fault", "killed" or "terminated by a signal"; not for an
       interrupt or a broken pipe).

       A list is a sequence of pipelines separated by:
       ;      run the pipelines one after the other
       &      run the pipeline before it in the background: the shell
              prints the pid of its last command and does not wait
       &&     run the next pipeline only if the last status was 0
       ||     run the next pipeline only if the last status was not 0
       && and || have equal precedence and group from the left; a
       pipeline that is skipped leaves the status as it was, so
       a && b || c runs c if a or b fails. A newline ends a list
       like ;. A word beginning with # starts a comment that runs to
       the end of the line.

       REDIRECTION

       Anywhere in a simple command:
       < file  take the standard input from file
       > file  send the standard output to file, created or truncated
               (mode 0666)
       >> file append the standard output to file, creating it if
               need be
       n> file, n>> file, n< file
               the same for descriptor n (0-9): 2> errors sends the
               error output to the file errors
       n>&m    make descriptor n a copy of m: 2>&1 sends errors where
               the output goes; >&2 sends the output to the error
               output
       Redirections apply left to right, so cmd > log 2>&1 puts both
       in log, while cmd 2>&1 > log sends errors to where the output
       went before (the terminal). In a pipeline the pipe is set up
       first: cmd 2>&1 | more pages both. Up to 8 redirections a
       command. A redirection with no command (> file, or : > file)
       creates or truncates the file. If a file cannot be opened the
       shell says why and the command is not run (status 1).

       <<WORD  a here-document: the lines after the command, up to a
               line holding only WORD, are its standard input. $ and
               `...` in them are expanded, unless WORD is quoted
               (<<'WORD'), which takes them literally; <<-WORD also
               drops leading tabs. Up to 16 in one line.

       QUOTING

       '...'  everything up to the next ' is taken literally
       "..."  literal, except that $ and `...` are expanded and \
              takes the next character literally; the result is one
              word and is not matched against file names
       \c     outside quotes, the character c is taken literally
       Quotes may be joined to other text in one word: a'b c'd is
       the single word "ab cd". An empty "" is an empty argument.

       VARIABLES

       A command made only of words of the form NAME=value sets those
       variables. Before a command (NAME=value cmd) they are set for
       that command alone. Every variable is in the environment, so
       all of them are passed to the commands the shell runs; HOME,
       PATH, USER and SHELL come from login(1). The values are
       substituted wherever they appear outside single quotes:
       $NAME, ${NAME}
              the value of the variable, or nothing if it is unset; a
              name is a letter or _ followed by letters, digits and _
       $0     the script's name (in an interactive shell, the name
              it was started by, such as sh or /bin/sh)
       $1 - $9
              the script's arguments (nothing if there are fewer)
       $#     the number of arguments
       $*     the arguments $1 ..., separated by single blanks
       $?     the status of the last command
       $$     the process id of the shell
       $!     the pid of the last background command
       A $ followed by anything else is left as it is. Outside double
       quotes a substituted value is split into words at its blanks,
       so for i in $* goes through the arguments one by one, and an
       empty or unset $X gives no word at all. Inside "..." it stays
       one word ("$X" is an empty argument when X is empty). The
       value of an assignment (x=$y) is never split.

       COMMAND SUBSTITUTION

       `command` is replaced by the standard output of command, run
       by a copy of the shell, with each newline turned into a blank
       and trailing blanks removed. Outside double quotes the result
       is split into words at its blanks (an empty result gives no
       word); inside them it stays one word. Example:
              n=`expr $n + 1`

       FILE NAME GENERATION

       An unquoted word containing *, ? or [ is a pattern, replaced
       by the sorted list of file names that match it:
       *      any string, including the empty string
       ?      any single character
       [...]  any one of the enclosed characters; a-z is a range, and
              a leading ! or ^ matches any character not enclosed
       Only the last component of a path may hold a pattern (/usr/*
       works, /*/bin does not). A name beginning with . matches only
       if the pattern begins with . too. A pattern that matches
       nothing is left unchanged.

       CONTROL FLOW

       A keyword is recognized only as the first word of a command:
       at the start of a line or after ;, or after another keyword.

       if list; then list; [elif list; then list; ...] [else list;] fi
              runs the if list; if its status is 0 runs the then
              list, otherwise tries each elif in turn, then the else
              list. With no branch taken the status is 0. At most 8
              if and elif branches.
       while list; do list; done
              runs the do list as long as the while list has status
              0. The status afterwards is 0.
       for NAME in word ...; do list; done
              expands the words once (variables, `...` and file
              names), then runs the do list with NAME set to each in
              turn. The word in is required.
       case word in pattern [| pattern] ...) list ;; ... esac
              runs the list of the first pattern that matches word
              (expanded, not split). The patterns are those of FILE
              NAME GENERATION (*, ?, [...]), quoted text matches
              itself, and | separates alternatives; *) catches the
              rest. The status is the list's, or 0 if none matched.
       name() { list; }
              defines a function. Running name args then runs the
              list in this shell, with $1 ... and $# the arguments of
              the call; return [n] leaves it with status n (or the
              last status). Functions may call each other, up to 50
              deep.
       ( list )
              runs the list in a child shell: cd, variables and exit
              there do not affect this one.
       { list; }
              runs the list in this shell, as one command, so the
              group can be redirected or piped as a whole:
              { echo a; echo b; } > file
       break  leaves the innermost while or for loop.
       continue
              goes on with the next pass of the innermost loop.

       Each ; in the forms above may be a newline instead, so a
       construct can be written over several lines. A misplaced
       keyword is reported as "sh: syntax: unexpected keyword" and
       the whole construct is not run (status 2).

       BUILT-IN COMMANDS

       These are run by the shell itself, and only as a command on
       its own (not in a pipeline):
       . file run the commands in file in this shell (it can set
              variables and define functions)
       cd [dir]
              change the working directory to dir, or to $HOME (/ if
              HOME is unset)
       exit [n]
              leave the shell with status n, or the last status
       eval word ...
              run the words, joined by blanks, as a command line
       exec command [arg ...]
              run command in place of the shell (the shell ends)
       export NAME=value ...
              set the variables (as NAME=value does); arguments
              without = are ignored, since every variable is in the
              environment already. Status 0
       read NAME ...
              read one line from the standard input (read NAME <
              file reads from file) and set each NAME to a word of
              it, the last to the rest of the line. Status 1 at the
              end of the input
       return [n]
              leave the function being run (see CONTROL FLOW)
       set    list the variables
       set -e, set -x (+e, +x to turn off)
              -e: a command that fails (outside an if or while
              condition and the left of && or ||) ends the shell; -x:
              print each command line, after a +, before running it
       set [--] arg ...
              make the args $1 ...
       shift [n]
              drop the first n (1) arguments: $2 becomes $1
       umask [mask]
              show or set the file creation mask, in octal
       unset NAME ...
              remove the variables
       wait   wait for every background command to finish
       :      do nothing, status 0
       break, continue
              see CONTROL FLOW (outside a loop: an error, status 1)
       history
              list the commands in the history, numbered. It runs
              in a child process, so it works in a pipeline and with
              redirection: history | grep cc

       SIGNALS

       The interrupt (^C) and quit (^\) characters stop the command
       running in the foreground; the shell itself ignores them.
       Commands started with & ignore them too. At the prompt, ^C
       abandons the line being typed. A script run with sh file also
       ignores them and goes on with its next command; sh -c does
       not ignore them.

LINE EDITING
       When the standard input is a terminal the shell reads each
       line itself, so it can be edited before Return sends it.
       Printable characters are inserted at the cursor.
       ^B, left arrow
              move back one character
       ^F, right arrow
              move forward one character
       ^A, Home
              go to the start of the line
       ^E, End
              go to the end of the line
       backspace, DEL
              delete the character before the cursor
       ^D, Delete
              delete the character under the cursor; ^D on an empty
              line is the end of input, and the shell exits
       ^K     delete from the cursor to the end of the line
       ^U     delete the whole line
       ^W     delete the word (up to a blank) before the cursor
       ^L     redisplay the line on a new line
       Tab    complete the word before the cursor: a command name
              (built-ins and the programs on $PATH) for the first
              word of a command, else a file name (a directory gets
              a /). With several choices it fills in what they share
              and rings the bell; a second Tab lists them.
       ^P, up arrow
              replace the line with the previous command in the
              history
       ^N, down arrow
              the next command; past the newest, the line that was
              being typed
       ^C     abandon the line and prompt again
       Other control characters are ignored. The arrow keys, Home,
       End and Delete are the VT100 sequences (ESC [ A and so on).

HISTORY
       An interactive shell keeps the last 100 commands typed. A
       command the same as the one before it, and an empty line, are
       not kept. Each command is also appended to $HOME/.sh_history,
       and the shell reads that file when it starts, so the history
       survives logging out; when the file has grown past 200 lines
       it is cut back to the last 100. The commands are numbered from
       1 in each session.

       Before a line is run, these are replaced (not inside '...',
       and not after a \):
       !!     the last command
       !n     command number n
       !-n    the n-th last command (!-1 is !!)
       !text  the last command beginning with text (text ends at a
              blank, ;, | or &)
       The line that results is shown, then run. If no command
       matches, the shell says "sh: !text: event not found" and runs
       nothing. A ! followed by anything else, such as a blank or =,
       is left alone, so test a != b works.

EXAMPLES
       ls -l /bin | more
       cc -o hello hello.c && ./hello
       grep -n main *.c > /tmp/out &
       for f in *.c; do echo $f; wc -l $f; done
       if test -d /mnt/usr; then echo mounted; fi
       case $1 in -v) verbose=1 ;; *) file=$1 ;; esac
       cat > note <<END
       made by $USER
       END
       i=0; while [ $i -lt 5 ]; do i=`expr $i + 1`; done
       sh -c 'cd /usr/ken; ls'

FILES
       $HOME/.sh_history  the command history
       /etc/profile, $HOME/.profile
                          run by a login shell
       /bin, /usr/bin     where commands are looked for

ENVIRONMENT
       HOME   for cd with no argument, and the history file
       PATH   the directories searched for commands

EXIT STATUS
       The status of the last command run; for exit n, n. 1 if the
       script file cannot be opened; 2 if the -c text is too long.

SEE ALSO
       test(1), expr(1), login(1), su(1), echo(1), true(1), false(1),
       kill(1), exec(2), fork(2)

BUGS
       There is no until, trap or $@, no PS1, and no job control.
       wait takes no pid: it waits for every background command;
       exec with no command (exec > file) does nothing.

       Words are split at spaces only (there is no IFS: a tab inside
       a value is not a separator), and a word that joins quoted
       text to an unquoted $X ("a b"$X) is split at the quoted
       space too. export with a bare NAME does nothing (every
       variable is exported already).

       The $0 of sh -c is the command text itself, and $1 the first
       argument after it.

       Redirection of a whole loop (done > file) is not possible;
       put the loop in { ...; } > file instead.

       Scripts run by name through the C library (execve(2) falls
       back to the #! interpreter or sh), not the kernel, so a
       setuid script is not possible.

       Limits: lines of 1024 bytes (longer script lines are split),
       a construct of 4096 bytes, 255 arguments to a command (more
       is an error, "more than 255 arguments"; a program can be
       given 250, with 4 KiB of argument text in all), 512 words
       in a for list, 16 KiB of names from wildcards in one line
       (more is an error, "too many names"), 16 commands to a
       pipeline, 512 bytes of `...` command text.
